<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">After a difficult start to the season, Not=
ts County had won four and drawn one of their previous five games when they=
 found themselves a player short for the league game against Aston Villa on=
 9 November 1889. They turned to their neighbors Nottingham Forest, who wer=
e not yet a league club, and borrowed their center-forward Tinsley Lindley,=
 who had scored a hat-trick on his debut as a 16-year-old seven years earli=
er. Lindley, who always wore walking shoes rather than football boots, insi=
sting he could run quicker in them, also scored 14 goals in 13 appearances =
for England. He didn=E2=80=99t score against Villa in the 1-1 draw, but Vil=
la protested, and County were docked a point and fined =C2=A35.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">The Cambridge-educated Lindley, though, wa=
s also a qualified barrister and he took charge of County=E2=80=99s appeal.=
 To dock them a point and fine them, Lindley argued, was to punish them twi=
ce for the same offense. The league agreed =E2=80=93 sort of. The point was=
 restored but County=E2=80=99s fine was increased to =C2=A325. County won j=
ust twice more in the league that season, finishing 10th in the 12-team lea=
gue and having to apply for re-election. Villa came eighth.</p>
